 license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
 metadata:
   project: https://codeberg.org/pojntfx/phial-neo
@@ -8,23 +8,24 @@ ---
 
 # phial guest
 
-You run inside the phial guest. The host is a Fedora Workstation machine that runs this guest under QEMU. The host is trusted. The guest is not trusted. The host user decides which secrets enter the guest and for how long.
+You run inside the phial guest. The host is a Fedora Workstation machine that runs this guest under QEMU. The host is trusted. The guest is not trusted. The host user decides which secrets and keys the guest may use, for how long, and for which machine.
 
 ## Terms
 
 - The host runs the program phial and the virtual machine.
 - The guest is the virtual machine that you run in.
-- Env mode puts a secret in a file in the secrets share.
-- Proxy mode keeps the secret on the host and injects it into an outbound request there.
-- A grant is a time box on one secret.
+- A secret is a value in the keyring of the host. Env mode puts a secret in a file in the secrets share. Proxy mode keeps the secret on the host and injects it into an outbound request there.
+- A key lives in the SSH agent of the session or in the GnuPG agent of the host. A guest never holds a private key.
+- A grant is a time box on one secret or key for one machine, or a single use of it.
+- The gate is the program on the host that enforces the grants. Every transport reaches the gate, and the gate asks the host user before it signs.
 
 ## What you can use
 
 - The runner: phial run loads every file in /mnt/phial-secrets as an environment variable and runs a command. Example: phial run env.
-- The proxy: send API requests to http://host.internal:4000/<name>. The host replaces your placeholder token with the real secret. Use the literal token phial-proxy-placeholder.
-- The SSH agent: when a grant is open, SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at the forwarded host agent. The host asks before the key signs.
-- The GnuPG agent: when a grant is open, gpg in the guest reaches the host agent. The host shows the pinentry prompt.
-- The launcher: the host runs the virtual machine as the user unit phial-vm.service.
+- The proxy: send API requests to http://host.internal:4000/<name>. The host adds the Authorization header with the real secret. Use the literal token phial-proxy-placeholder where your own placeholder belongs.
+- The SSH agent: SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at the gate. While a grant is open, the key is listed and the host user approves each signature. Without a grant the agent is empty.
+- The GnuPG agent: gpg in the guest reaches the agent of the host through the gate. While a grant is open, the host user approves the connection and the host pinentry asks for the passphrase when it must.
+- The launcher: the host runs the virtual machine as the user unit phial-vm@<name>.service.
 - A guest app can show in two places. Pick one on every run.
 - Waypipe mode shows one app window on the host. The guest runs the waypipe server on the display name waypipe. Run the app with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=waypipe.
 - Session mode shows the app inside the remote desktop session. The guest session owns the display name wayland-0. Run the app with WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0.
@@ -33,20 +34,27 @@ - Never use the display name 0 for waypipe. The desktop session already owns wayland-0, so the window lands in the remote desktop instead of on the host.
 - The MCP tool display.runApp carries a mode field. Send waypipe for the host, or session for the remote desktop.
 - The serial console: for kernel work, read the guest console from the host with socat - UNIX-CONNECT:$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial/vm/console.sock.
 
+## Asking the host
+
+The agent channel speaks MCP at http://host.internal:8080/mcp. The host knows which machine you are from the vsock address of the connection.
+
+- Read the state: secrets.ls, keys.ls, grants.ls, vm.ls. These never return a value or private material.
+- Ask for access: requests.grant opens a grant after the host user approves. It carries a target, a duration such as 30min, or once for a single use.
+- Ask for a change: requests.setSecret, requests.removeSecret, requests.generateSshKey, requests.generateGpgKey, requests.removeSshKey, requests.removeGpgKey. The host user approves or denies every one.
+- A request that nobody answers within five minutes counts as denied.
+
 ## Rules that you must follow
 
-- Never print a secret value. Print secret names only. The command phial ls lists names and never values.
+- Never print a secret value. Print secret names only. The tool secrets.ls lists names and never values.
 - Never read /mnt/phial-secrets directly when a runner or a unit can load the value for you.
-- Never run phial sync, phial allow, or phial revoke. Only the host user grants access.
-- Never try to add a provider or extend a grant. Ask the host user instead.
 - Never copy a private key into the guest. The guest holds no private keys.
-- A grant expires on time. If a proxy request returns 403, the grant is closed. Tell the host user.
+- A grant expires on time. If a proxy request returns 403, the grant is closed. Ask the host user for a new grant with requests.grant.
 
 ## How the system works
 
-- The host writes one file per secret into the secrets share. The guest mounts the share read-only at /mnt/phial-secrets.
+- The host writes one file per granted env secret into the secrets directory of this machine. The guest mounts it read-only at /mnt/phial-secrets.
 - The path unit phial-secrets.path restarts the units that consume secrets when the share changes.
 - Guest units load secrets with LoadCredential= so the unit does not see the environment.
-- The host proxy Caddy listens on the host loopback. A socat pair over vsock carries it to the guest.
-- The host injects the Authorization header after it matches the grant file for the row.
-- SSH reaches the guest over vsock. The host entry is ssh phial. No network device is needed.
+- The gate on the host listens on vsock. The guest reaches it through local sockets: SSH_AUTH_SOCK at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial/ssh-agent.sock, the GnuPG agent at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gnupg/S.gpg-agent, and the proxy at host.internal:4000.
+- The gate checks the grant for the name and for the machine before it answers, and it asks the host user before it signs.
+- SSH reaches the guest over vsock. The host entry is ssh <name>. No network device is needed.
